<p>Today's brief covers stories from 5 to 6 November.</p>

<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Rules</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>California and Canada<em>child online safety</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>AI agents</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Claude Dispatch research<em>browser extension to desktop agent</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Capacity</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Microsoft and Oracle<em>older GPUs retired</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Base images</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">OpenSSH on Alpine<em>exploited password bypass</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Two of today's stories are about trust boundaries: the smallest base image and the browser next to your agent.</figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="block">
<h2>1. An OpenSSH flaw on Alpine Linux is exploited within hours</h2>
<p>CVE-2026-123158, nicknamed "sshole", lets attackers log in over SSH without a password to certain accounts on Linux systems built on the musl C library without PAM, most notably Alpine Linux; OpenSSH released a fix in version 10.6p2 on 5 November<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Help Net Security reports exploitation began within hours of disclosure, mainly against accounts with no usable password, such as key-only users<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. glibc-based distributions such as Debian, Ubuntu and Fedora are reported as not affected<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> Alpine is everywhere in containers, appliances and cloud images, often in places nobody thinks of as "a server". Find every Alpine or other musl-based system that runs sshd, patch and rebuild images, and in the meantime disable password authentication or SSH entirely where it is not needed. Longer term, containers should not run SSH at all, and management access belongs on a separate, tightly controlled path; see <a href="/case-studies/us-utility-management-plane-hardening/">hardening a management plane</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>2. Researchers show a browser extension steering Claude Desktop through Dispatch</h2>
<p>Security researchers reported that a malicious or compromised browser extension with access to claude.ai could use Claude's Dispatch feature to make Claude Desktop run commands on a connected computer, which they demonstrated in testing<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. They reported no attacks in the wild; coverage differs on whether and how Anthropic will change the feature<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> once a desktop agent can act on a machine, everything that can talk to that agent is part of its attack surface, including browser extensions. Manage extensions with an allowlist on company browsers, keep human approval for agents running commands locally, and avoid automatic approval settings on machines with sensitive access. See <a href="/insights/agentic-infrastructure-skills-tools-sandboxes/">agentic infrastructure</a> and <a href="/insights/prompt-injection-defence-for-agents/">prompt injection defence for agents</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>3. Microsoft winds down rented AI capacity from Oracle</h2>
<p>Microsoft is winding down a 2023 arrangement to rent AI computing from Oracle, reportedly because the capacity ran on older Nvidia chips that no longer met its needs, The Next Web reports, citing Bloomberg<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. Oracle said demand for its AI infrastructure remains strong, including for earlier-generation GPUs<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> AI hardware ages fast, and a contract for a specific chip generation can become poor value well before it ends. When you reserve capacity, prefer terms tied to performance or allowing upgrades, keep commitments shorter than the hardware's useful life, and keep workloads able to move. See <a href="/insights/ai-capacity-crunch-cloud-planning/">planning around the AI capacity crunch</a> and the <a href="/case-studies/us-saas-multi-region-ai-capacity/">multi-region AI capacity case study</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>4. Google, Meta and TikTok sue to block California's youth social media law</h2>
<p>Google, Meta and TikTok filed separate lawsuits to block a California law, due to take effect on 1 January 2027, that restricts features such as addictive feeds, endless scrolling and autoplay for users under 16, arguing it violates the First Amendment<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> whatever the courts decide, the direction of travel is clear: age-aware product behaviour is becoming a legal requirement in more places. Products with younger users should be able to switch features on or off by age and jurisdiction through configuration, not code changes. See <a href="/insights/new-york-youth-chatbot-law-ai-assistants/">New York's youth chatbot law</a> and the <a href="/fractional-cto-united-states/">US page</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>5. Canada funds a new online safety regulator covering AI chatbots</h2>
<p>Canada's Budget 2026, released on 5 November, proposes $176.3 million over five years for a Digital Safety and Data Protection Commission<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>. BetaKit reports the commission would enforce proposed legislation covering social media and AI chatbot services, with powers including binding orders, audits and fines<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-8">8</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> AI chatbots are now named directly in online safety regimes, not just social networks. If you offer a chatbot to the public in Canada, start recording how you assess and reduce risks of harmful content, especially for younger users; the same evidence will serve in other markets. See <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd do this week</h2>
<ul>
<li>Inventory Alpine and other musl-based systems running sshd, patch to OpenSSH 10.6p2 and rebuild images.</li>
<li>Review browser extension policies on machines that run desktop AI agents.</li>
<li>Check reserved AI capacity contracts for chip generation and upgrade terms.</li>
<li>Make age and jurisdiction a configuration in consumer products.</li>
</ul>
<p>I am based in India, the United States and the UAE, working globally. <a href="/#assess">Analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
